Dear Stephen, & list:

WRT windows NT on Alpha machines:

There is no list anywhere for NT on alpha, not enough demand I guess.
The i86 guys mostly haven't a clue when it comes to Alpha issues.
Yeah, it's off-topic but I don't mind helping out ...

WRT to NT for Alpha - I run it, as well as Tru64 and Linux. NT on
alpha is unusually fast, shocking to someone who is used to runnung MS
products on i86 kit.  There are a fair number of native 64-bit
programs for it, and it will run most 32-bit windows programs by way
of an emulator called FX!32.  The only game I've tried on it is Riven,
which runs OK.

The AlphaPCs mostly came with AlphaBios but many have a bootloader
called MILO.  (To run Linux you use MILO as the main loader.) You need
to get to what is called the ARC console - if you have it.  Some of
the PC boards only had room for one console in the ROM.  If that's the
case, you may have to flash in AlphaBios. Try hitting F2 at your
console prompt and see what happens. If you can get in you should have
an "install NT" utility.

A manual can be downloaded from

http://www.microway.com/downloads/LX/LX_USER.pdf

you can download a fresh AlphaBios fom

http://www.microway.com/downloads/LX/


The standard NT 4.0 distro included both Alpha and i86 on a single CD.
Make sure you have your key code.  If you have to buy it, don't pay
over $40 new. NT alpha drivers, service packs, software and
documentation are disappearing fast, but a lot of it can be found at

ftp://dutlbcz.lr.tudelft.nl/alphant/

There is a beta version of windows2000 for Alpha that has escaped into
the wild. It is a server version and has some nice features, but lacks
that lightning-fast quality that is unique to NT 4.0 on alpha.

If you like your Alpha, I would recommend looking for a better
machine.  The best deal right now is the "Miata" class of 433, 500 and
600 mhz "personal workstations."  The best ones are the "gl" models
with the USB ports on the back.  I just got a late model PWS500 gl on
eBay for $157, shipping included.

I don't use NT much at all, except to run my CAD program, ArcView,
Corel Draw and WordPerfect.  And I absolutely will NOT go on-line with
any Microsoft product.

I think that the best thing to say about NT on alpha is you don't have
to keep a PC around to run windows applications.

Well, good luck to you Stephen, enjoy your Alpha.  

jn






